<article class="env-lemma" id="00TC">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00TC">Lemma <span data-tag="00TC">10.137.12</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Then $R \to S$ is smooth if and only if $R \to S$ is smooth at every prime $\mathfrak q$ of $S$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The direct implication is trivial. Suppose that $R \to S$ is smooth at every prime $\mathfrak q$ of $S$. Since $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S)$ is quasi-compact, see Lemma <a href="/tag/00E8" data-tag="00E8">10.17.8</a>, there exists a finite covering $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(S) = \bigcup D(g_ i)$ such that each $S_{g_ i}$ is smooth. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00EP" data-tag="00EP">10.23.3</a> this implies that $S$ is of finite presentation over $R$. According to Lemma <a href="/tag/00S7" data-tag="00S7">10.134.13</a> we see that $\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{S/R} \otimes _ S S_{g_ i}$ is quasi-isomorphic to a finite projective $S_{g_ i}$-module placed in degree $0$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00NX" data-tag="00NX">10.78.2</a> this implies that $\mathop{N\! L}\nolimits _{S/R}$ is quasi-isomorphic to a finite projective $S$-module placed in degree $0$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>